{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Speak, etc. and you shall say, etc.</b> The reason for the doubling is that in this commandment there are two matters: the prevention of working the land, and the making available of the produce that grows from the trees and the vegetation of the ground. Corresponding to the prevention of working the land and the forestalling of its success, He said <b>דַּבֵּר</b> — an expression of hardness, for it is a thing that a person does not desire, since it holds back good. And corresponding to the making available of what grows, He said <b>וְאָמַרְתָּ</b>, for it is a thing in which everyone rejoices, that one's hand may reach for all. Also, on account of the promises He commanded concerning the matter (Leviticus 25:21): ״וְעָשָׂת אֶת הַתְּבוּאָה לִשְׁלֹשׁ הַשָּׁנִים״ — therefore He said ״וְאָמַרְתָּ״, an utterance that comforts the heart." "<b>Further,</b> it intends that since this commandment of the sabbath of the land contains two matters: the first, the decree of a supreme King upon His servants — an obligation to fulfill every matter; and the second, that even though this matter is between a man and his fellow, he is obligated to act accordingly by law — for one who gives a gift to his fellow and stipulates a condition at the time of the gift, his condition is binding. So too the Master, blessed be He, at the time of the gift stipulates conditions, as He said ״אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי נֹתֵן״ — and He did not say ״אֲשֶׁר נָתַתִּי.״ Therefore, corresponding to the decree of a supreme King, He said ״דַּבֵּר,״ and corresponding to the matter being required by law, He said ״וְאָמַרְתָּ״ — meaning, even without the force of a royal decree they would accept the utterance." "<b>And the land shall keep a sabbath, etc.</b> One must know what the verse intends with this utterance. If it concerns the sabbatical year, behold He says immediately afterward (Leviticus 25:4): ״וּבַשָּׁנָה הַשְּׁבִיעִית שַׁבַּת שַׁבָּתוֹן״." "<b>Indeed,</b> HaShem intended to say, adjacent to the mention of His giving them the land, that there is a reservation for HaShem in the land. That is His saying <b>אֲנִי נֹתֵן</b> — and not an outright gift, but rather <b>וְשָׁבְתָה</b>, etc., <b>לַה׳</b>. He then went back and specified the measure of this sabbath — whether a month, whether a year, after how many years — and said <b>שֵׁשׁ שָׁנִים וּבַשָּׁנָה הַשְּׁבִיעִית שַׁבָּת</b>, etc. From this it follows that had the verse not said ״וְשָׁבְתָה״, etc., but only what it said afterward — ״וּבַשָּׁנָה הַשְּׁבִיעִית״ — I would not have known that HaShem reserved this year for Himself; rather, I would have thought He gave an outright gift in perpetuity. But it is not so." "<b>And I have observed,</b> and I see that Elohim accounted it for the good in the arrangement of this matter. For if HaShem had given the land on condition, then upon the nullification of the condition the gift would be nullified; whereas in that He made a reservation, when they lay hand upon the reservation it is not for this reason that the gift is nullified — rather, they must pay everything that accrues from the reservation. And the faithful witness is what the verse says (Leviticus 26:34): ״אָז תִּרְצֶה הָאָרֶץ אֶת שַׁבְּתוֹתֶיהָ אֵת אֲשֶׁר לֹא שָׁבְתָה״, etc. From this it follows that there is no nullification of the gift of the land forever." "<b>Further</b>, the reason for the juxtaposition of ״וְשָׁבְתָה״ to ״אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי נוֹתֵן״ appears [to be], according to their words that they said (Bava Metzi'a 23a): the floor of a sage is in place of an oath. And the Sage from whom utterances of wisdom come forth is YHWH Elohim, and therefore when He brought forth from the mouth of the Most High ״אֲנִי נוֹתֵן״ — His saying it is in place of an oath. Or it may also mean that since HaShem's gift to Israel has the legal status of charity, its utterance has the legal status of a vow (Rosh HaShanah 6a), and therefore immediately, straightway, within the time of speech of saying ״אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי נוֹתֵן״ He said ״וְשָׁבְתָה הָאָרֶץ״ etc. — and were it not for this, what He said afterward, ״וּבַשָּׁנָה הַשְּׁבִיעִית״ etc., would not be within the time of speech of the utterance of giving, for behold, He interrupted with the utterance ״שֵׁשׁ שָׁנִים״ etc., ״וְשֵׁשׁ שָׁנִים״ etc."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:01:11.810621586Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}